meta {
  name: Get status
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/health/status
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.status: eq ok
  res.body.name: eq api
  res.body.version: isDefined
  res.body.date: isDefined
  res.body.requestId: isDefined
}

docs {
  The verdict plus everything needed to say which process gave it, from `StatusUseCase`:

  ```json
  {
    "status": "ok",
    "name": "api",
    "version": "0.1.0",
    "date": "2026-08-06T09:41:12.004Z",
    "requestId": "not-yet-wired"
  }
  ```

  Meant for a person, which is why it is not what the probes call — the three extra fields
  are the ones a bug report starts with, and none of them are worth a round trip once a
  second.

  `version` comes from `npm_package_version`, so a `0.0.0` here means the process was
  started by something that does not export it (`node dist/main.js`, `pnpm exec`, or a pod
  whose spec is missing the pair) rather than that a release went out as `0.0.0`.

  `date` is generated per call, in UTC — held against your own clock it also shows how far
  the two have drifted.

  `requestId` is the literal `not-yet-wired` until request ids exist; the assertions pin
  the fields that are real and only check that this one is present.
}
